export default "## Two Genuinely Private Options\n\nIf you're choosing between paper and an on-device digital tracker, you're already in the category of people who've decided that privacy matters for this data. That's the right starting point. Both paper and on-device digital are meaningfully different from cloud-based period tracking, and it's worth being clear about exactly what each option offers.\n\n**Paper tracking** is the floor of digital privacy risk because it has no digital surface area. No breach, no subpoena directed at a company, no privacy policy, no algorithm. What you write stays where you put it. The vulnerabilities are physical: someone finding the notebook, losing it, or the paper degrading.\n\n**Local-first digital tracking** (Floriva) reduces server-side risk while adding the functionality that paper can't: pattern analysis across cycles, symptom correlation, BBT charting, reminders, and structured export. The tradeoff is that a device, phone or tablet, is more likely to be searched than a notebook in some circumstances. App lock mitigates this.\n\n## What You Actually Get With Floriva Over Paper\n\nThe practical difference shows up when you have several months of data and want to understand it. With paper, you have a stack of filled-out pages and need to calculate averages manually, graph BBT by hand, and correlate symptoms yourself. With Floriva, the same data produces a cycle history view, symptom trend analysis, and a structured record you can export for a doctor.\n\nFor anyone tracking symptoms for a clinical purpose, endometriosis documentation, PCOS management, heavy bleeding records for a gynecologist, the difference is significant. Paper requires discipline and manual analysis that many people abandon after a few months. Digital with good logging habits produces records that are actually usable in an appointment.\n\n## The Cloud Gap\n\nThe more important privacy distinction in period tracking is not paper versus digital. It is [on-device versus cloud-based](/resources/guides/on-device-storage-period-tracker/). Cloud-based period apps store your cycle history on their servers. That data is governed by their privacy policy, which can change. It can be reached by legal process. And as the [FTC action against Flo established](/compare/alternatives/flo-app-alternative/), it can be shared with advertising platforms.\n\n[Period tracker data is not covered by HIPAA](/resources/guides/period-tracker-hipaa/). There is no federal law preventing a period app from sharing your cycle data with Facebook. The FTC enforcement was under FTC Act Section 5, deceptive practices, not a health privacy framework. The gap between what users expect and what apps do with their data is documented.\n\nPaper avoids this gap entirely. Floriva's local-first architecture avoids the readable server-side portion of this gap while adding digital utility. Cloud-based apps require trusting the company with years of your reproductive health data.";
